For every natural J>=2 and real h>0, index the J nodes by i in Fin(J)={0,...,J-1} and define
  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),
where i and J are cast to real numbers in the real quotient. Define the real Lagrange basis polynomial
  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J), k!=j} (X-t_k)/(t_j-t_k).
Equivalently, before injectivity is established, each denominator is interpreted using real inversion in the polynomial product specified by the context. Define
  Lambda=sup_{s in [0,h]} sum_{j in Fin(J)} |ell_j(s)|,
  omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i} (t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds,
  v_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds.
Then all of the following hold:
  t_i belongs to [0,h] for every i;
  i -> t_i is injective;
  t_{J-1}=h;
  ell_i(t_j)=1 if i=j and 0 otherwise, for every i,j;
  sum_j ell_j(s)=1 for every real s, including outside [0,h];
  1<=Lambda;
  sum_j |omega_ij| <= (t_i^2/2)Lambda for every i;
  sum_j |omega_ij| <= (h^2/2)Lambda for every i;
  sum_j v_j=h.
The supremum is the conditionally complete real supremum of the displayed image of the closed interval. Integrals are oriented Lebesgue interval integrals; the node conclusion ensures their displayed endpoints are in nonnegative increasing order. All sums and products use the J actual indices. The theorem gives these interpolation, normalization and absolute-row-bound properties; it asserts no upper asymptotic bound on Lambda, positivity of individual integral weights, general polynomial quadrature exactness, probability kernel, or sampling convergence property.
